    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d217">

                <pb facs="0297" n="287" sameAs="frus:frus1944v02/pg287"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">840.48/9–2644: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in Sweden</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-10-13T22:00:00-04:00">October 13,
                            1944—10 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2058. Infotel. At instance of this Government the question of relaxing blockade
                    to permit limited relief shipments to Allied territory remaining under enemy
                    occupation was recently taken up in London. There seems to be reason to believe
                    that it will be possible to arrange for a modification in that policy but final
                    clearance especially with the military authorities has not yet been received. At
                    this time pending further developments it is not possible to give a definitive
                    reply on the proposals set forth in your 3883, September 26.<anchor n="59"
                        xml:id="d217-f1"/> Legation will be kept informed of developments. Whisler
                    may be informed in confidence of foregoing.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d217-f1" n="59" xml:id="d217-f1-content">
                <p>In this telegram the Minister reported on discussions he had held with Norwegian
                    and Swedish relief officials concerning relief activities in Norway. On the
                    basis of these discussions he concluded that distribution of relief supplies in
                    Norway was being carried out with a minimum of interference from the Germans and
                    that he was therefore recommending that a more liberal policy respecting the
                    licensing of shipments be adopted; he also recommended that additional supplies
                    of food and clothing be forwarded from the United States. (840.48/9–2644)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
